{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_23","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/6/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"878187ab\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nReplace the scattered foreground markers with a bottom-left count rectangle whose two corner diagonals encode the marker counts.\n\nInput structure\n- A background grid with exactly two foreground colors used as scattered marker pixels.\n- The two foreground colors have distinct pixel counts.\n\nRule\n- Ignore marker positions and use only the two foreground counts.\n- Make a 16-by-16 background canvas, fill a bottom-left red rectangle whose height is the smaller count and width is the larger count.\n- Paint yellow pixels along the two rising 45-degree diagonals from the rectangle's bottom-left and bottom-right corners.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker colors and positions vary between examples; their counts alone determine the output rectangle dimensions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"332202d5\" lines=\"49-64\">\n<description>\nFill every row from the nearest marked horizontal bar, turning bar rows and color-boundary ties into separator rows.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background fills the grid except for full-width horizontal bars and one vertical marker column.\n- Outside the marker column, every row is monochrome: either background or a source bar color.\n- Background rows share one marker color in the marker column; source bar rows share the other.\n\nRule\n- Each non-source row takes the color of the nearest source bar, with that row's marker-column pixel set to the source-marker color.\n- If a row is exactly midway between nearest bars of different colors, make the whole row the source-marker color.\n- Each original source bar row becomes a source-marker row with the marker-column pixel changed to the background-row marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The marked bars are horizontal seeds for a one-dimensional nearest-seed partition over rows; equal-distance rows are separators only when the competing seed colors differ.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\" lines=\"36-55\">\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f28a3cbb\" lines=\"42-50\">\n<description>\nCompress each colored ray pixel inward until it touches that color's 3x3 anchor block.\nInput structure\n- A uniform background grid with non-background colors.\n- Each foreground color contains one solid 3x3 anchor block; all other pixels of that color lie on straight 8-neighbor rays aimed at that block.\nRule\n- Keep anchor blocks fixed, erase loose foreground pixels, and for each ray pack its pixels into consecutive cells adjacent to the anchor along the same ray.\nKey insight\n- The scattered pixels are displaced pixels on visible row, column, or diagonal sightlines back to their same-colored 3x3 block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e376de54\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nAlign parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.\n- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.\n- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.\n- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.\n\nKey insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc10701f\" lines=\"20-35\">\n<description>\nMove the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7666fa5d\" lines=\"12-26\">\n<description>\nFill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).\n- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.\n\nRule:\n- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.\n- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).\n- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).\n\nKey insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"66ac4c3b\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\n\nRule\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"320afe60\" lines=\"41-56\">\n<description>\nSlide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\nRule:\n- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.\n- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"17b866bd\" lines=\"49-67\">\n<description>\nMove each marker from a lattice corner gap into the adjacent enclosed 12-pixel cavity.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a repeated teal lattice around black 12-pixel cavities.\n- A marker is a non-black pixel placed at the northwest corner gap of one\n  complete 5x5 lattice tile; the marker may itself be teal.\n\nRule:\n- For every marked northwest corner gap, clear that marker to black.\n- Fill the 12 black pixels in the tile immediately down-right of the marker\n  with the marker color.\n- Leave the rest of the lattice unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The apparent isolated markers are control pixels for the adjacent cavity;\n  teal markers are recognized as extra teal pixels occupying a gap that the\n  periodic lattice template says should be black.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"985ae207\" lines=\"82-97\">\n<description>\nRepeat each 3x3 control-center sprite toward the matching solid rectangle until a copy first overlaps that rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Each sprite is a 3x3 foreground object whose eight border pixels have one color and whose center has a different control color.\n- For every control color, there is exactly one separate solid rectangular object of that color.\n\nRule\n- Match each sprite to the unique solid rectangle whose color equals the sprite center.\n- Copy the whole 3x3 sprite in the cardinal direction of that rectangle, stepping by three cells each time.\n- Stop after pasting the first copy whose 3x3 bbox overlaps the rectangle; copied pixels overwrite the rectangle there.\n\nKey insight\nThe center pixel is not the paint color: it is a destination key, while the entire 3x3 sprite is the motif tiled along the straight path.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e3721c99\" lines=\"69-83\">\n<description>\nRecolor grey shapes by matching their hole count to a color legend.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
